 0.036468505859375, -0.0241851806640625, -0.02593994140625, -0.004863739013671875, -0.0028285980224609375, 0.004425048828125, 0.00919342041015625, 0.01055908203125, -0.0031147003173828125, -0.0013341903686523438, -0.0012407302856445312, -0.0163421630859375, 0.0252532958984375, 0.0099029541015625, 0.03369140625, 0.0011501312255859375, 0.0245513916015625, -0.018951416015625, 0.0208892822265625, 0.008941650390625, 0.004810333251953125, 0.0214996337890625, -0.01593017578125, 0.013885498046875, 0.00856781005859375, -0.0087127685546875, -0.027435302734375, 0.02484130859375, 0.0103607177734375, 0.018768310546875, -0.02197265625, -0.00024771690368652344, -0.01361846923828125, -0.0030956268310546875, 0.0311431884765625, -0.009307861328125, 0.023162841796875, -0.0034046173095703125, -0.01470947265625, 0.0026111602783203125, -0.027069091796875, -0.0162353515625, 0.021148681640625, -0.007717132568359375, 0.0220184326171875, -0.00522613525390625, 0.02783203125, 0.0297698974609375, -0.00759124755859375, -0.0118255615234375, -0.00913238525390625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose and Rationale:\n\n**Background:** High-quality, structured radiology reporting is critical for clinical care, interpretation, and auditing. Despite widespread recognition, many reports still rely on non-standardized language, anatomical ambiguity, and incomplete documentation, undermining QA and workflow efficiency.  \n\n**Prior Limitations:** Existing solutions, such as strictly rule-based sectioning tools and single-agent large language models, have struggled to effectively process reports with complex, multi-organ findings. These tools often lack transparency, adaptability, or scalability (see Supplementary Table 1). Furthermore, dependence on cloud infrastructure or third-party data sources can impede local adaptation and privacy compliance.\n\n**System Innovation:** To address these limitations, we developed a multi-agent AI system that operates entirely on local infrastructure, combining deterministic anatomical parsing, a fine-tuned open-source language model, and interpretable QA logic. This hybrid architecture is designed to deliver robust free-text report structuring, surface clinically relevant inconsistencies, and ensure transparency and real-time adaptability to an institution\u2019s evolving needs.\n\nMaterials and Methods:\n\n**Data Cohort:**  \nA retrospective exploratory study was conducted using 638 CT radiology reports (chest, abdomen, pelvis) dictated in 2023\u20132024 by 15 board-certified radiologists at a single academic center.  \n- **Inclusion:** Adult patient age (>18 years), examination within study period, dictated by a study radiologist, and full availability of report text plus accompanying demographic metadata.  \n- **Exclusion:** Incomplete, pediatric, or heavily redacted reports.  \nSampling was random based on these criteria.\n\n**AI System Architecture:**  \n\n**1. Rule-Based Section Labeling Agent**  \n- **Lexicon and Regular Expressions:**  \n    - Lexicons of >50 anatomical entities were curated from ACR and RSNA vocabulary standards, then refined using both iterative keyword extraction (identifying additional synonyms/variants from a representative sample of 200 in-house reports) and manual correction by two radiologists.  \n    - Only terms with unambiguous anatomical meaning were included; ambiguous or overlapping synonyms were excluded based on consensus between the radiologist annotators.\n- **Section Prioritization:**  \n    - Each sentence was tokenized and searched for anatomical entities according to a predetermined hierarchy, with priority accorded to organs commonly listed first in standardized reports or recognized as most clinically significant for potential findings (e.g., \u201cliver\u201d prioritized over \u201ckidney\u201d if both are mentioned together), reflecting common clinical conventions at the institution. This hierarchy was established empirically from in-house templates and is provided in the supplement for reproducibility.\n    - Example: For the sentence \u201cThere is a hepatic mass extending into the upper pole of the right kidney,\u201d the label \u201cliver\u201d would be assigned, following the local reporting convention.\n    - Sentences matching multiple entities received the highest-priority single label; sentences with no lexicon matches were assigned \u201cmiscellaneous.\u201d  \n- **Output:** Anatomical section labels for each sentence, facilitating downstream structuring.\n\n**2. Locally Deployed Language Model Agent**  \n- **Selection/Preprocessing:**  \n    - Llama 2 13B was selected after benchmarking for accuracy and resource requirements (see Supplementary Table 2). Compared to smaller (7B, 3B) and cloud-based models, Llama 2 13B showed superior structuring fidelity and could be deployed on-site.  \n    - Training data consisted of 15,000 de-identified, non-overlapping CT report sections, preprocessed by removing named entities and mapping to canonical section labels; reports with <2 sentences or >15% redacted text were excluded.  \n- **Fine-Tuning and Validation:**  \n    - The model was fine-tuned (Adam optimizer, PyTorch v1.13, HuggingFace Transformers v4.32) using a learning rate of 1e-5, batch size 12, over 4 epochs. Batch size and epoch number were chosen based on GPU memory constraints and validation loss on a held-out set, balancing convergence with computational feasibility.  \n    - Model threshold for section assignment was set at >80% lexical overlap with labeled ground truth, based on pilot tuning for optimal precision/recall (see Figure S1).  \n    - Output: Structured, labeled reports mapped to predefined anatomical sections.\n\n**3. Deterministic QA Agent**  \n- **Operation:**  \n    - Explicit rules flagged three error types:  \n        - **Section mismatch:** Abnormalities such as \u201cmass\u201d or \u201cembolism\u201d cited in Findings but not referenced (including in negated/uncertain form) in Impression. Negation was handled via a NegEx-based pipeline.  \n        - **Gender-anatomy conflict:** Occurrence of gender-specific organs in mismatch with patient\u2019s gender (e.g., \u201cuterus\u201d in a male patient), after checking structured demographic metadata.  \n        - **Undocumented communication:** Terms like \u201cSTAT\u201d, \u201curgent\u201d, or \u201ccritical\u201d present with no documented notification of a provider, requiring the word \u201cnotified\u201d or equivalent string.  \n    - Rules were developed iteratively by radiologists reviewing real-world error cases; full definitions are in Supplementary Table S2.  \n- **Output:** Binary QA flags for each error category.\n\n**Evaluation and Sampling:**\n\nA stratified subset of reports was created for detailed review.  \n- Reports were categorized based on presence of each QA flag:  \n    - 80 with \u201csection mismatch\u201d  \n    - 5 with \u201cgender-anatomy conflict\u201d  \n    - 5 with \u201cundocumented communication\u201d  \n    - 553 unflagged  \n- From these, 45 were selected for review:  \n    - 20 randomly selected with section mismatch  \n    - 10 with gender-anatomy conflict  \n    - 10 with undocumented communication  \n    - 5 unflagged controls  \n    - Each report appeared only once by its primary error flag. Case selection flow is summarized in Figure 1.\n\n**Reviewer Scoring and Metrics:**  \n\n- Two board-certified abdominal radiologists (each >5 years\u2019 experience) independently reviewed cases.  \n- **Rubric (see Table 1):** Four metrics:  \n    - **Fidelity:** Accuracy of report structure preservation  \n    - **Accuracy:** Correctness of label assignment  \n    - **Omission:** Missing findings/abnormalities  \n    - **Fabrication:** Introduction of artifactual details  \n    - Metrics were scored as \u201cexcellent,\u201d \u201cgood,\u201d or \u201cfair.\u201d  \n- **Reviewer Blinding:**  \n    - Reviewers were blinded to error category, patient metadata (age, gender, study date, identifiers), and to each other\u2019s ratings.  \n    - Report order was randomized for each reviewer to minimize order effects and bias.\n- **Discrepancy Resolution:**  \n    - Initial discrepancies were discussed in joint sessions; if consensus was not reached, a senior radiologist adjudicated by reviewing only the relevant outputs, still blinded to group assignment.\n\n**Statistical Analysis:**  \n\n- Analyses were descriptive, as stratified oversampling (spectrum bias) artificially inflates error prevalence. Proportions are given with 95% Wilson confidence intervals (scipy.stats v1.10.1; code in supplement).  \n- Reliability was evaluated via Cohen\u2019s kappa (sklearn v1.2.2) for binary (\u201ccorrect/incorrect\u201d) and weighted for three-level ratings.\n- No hypothesis testing or p-values were calculated, as population inferential statistics were not appropriate.\n\nResults:\n\n**Overall Output:**  \nThe system processed all 638 reports (22,270 sentences). The QA agent flagged:  \n- Section mismatch: 80/638 (12.5%)  \n- Gender-anatomy conflict: 5/638 (0.8%)  \n- Undocumented communication: 5/638 (0.8%)\n\n**Illustrative Error Examples (summarized in Table 2):**  \n- *Section mismatch*: \u201cRenal mass\u201d described in Findings but not addressed or negated in Impression.  \n- *Gender-anatomy conflict*: \u201cProstate\u201d reported in a female patient\u2019s record.  \n- *Undocumented communication*: \u201cPulmonary embolism detected urgent\u201d with no documentation of provider notification.  \n- **Errors often missed:** Complex findings embedded in multi-organ descriptions occasionally led to partial sectioning (e.g., a sentence mentioning both liver and kidney with only \u201cliver\u201d retained due to prioritization rule).\n\n**Stratified Review Results (n=45, see Table 3):**  \n- 31/45 (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) excellent/good structuring  \n- 2/45 (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201312%) incorrect (compound findings misassigned due to hierarchy limitation)  \n- 12/45 (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%) required consensus resolution (mainly \u201cgood\u201d vs \u201cfair\u201d on label boundary)  \n- Cohen\u2019s kappa: 0.49; weighted: 0.55  \n- 40/45 reached consensus after discussion; 5 required adjudication  \n- 0/45 omission or fabrication events (upper 95% CI < 7%)  \n- Grading distribution: 17/45 (38%) \u201cexcellent,\u201d 21/45 (46%) \u201cgood,\u201d 7/45 (16%) \u201cfair\u201d  \n\n**Notable Limitations Observed:** Persistent errors traced to the deterministic, anatomy-prioritized hierarchy\u2014particularly for multi-organ statements (e.g., \u201cSplenic and hepatic lesions identified\u201d\u2014assigned only to \u201cspleen\u201d due to hierarchy). Some nuanced clinical misinterpretations\u2014such as subtle omissions in Impression if language was highly indirect\u2014were not flagged.\n\n**Standalone Comparison:**  \nNo commercial/open-source QA tool exists for this workflow; a pilot manual-only sectioning in 20 cases achieved 90% \u201cexcellent/good\u201d vs 84% by our system, indicating comparable structuring fidelity."}}